new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5a69348
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+## Deployment
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) config for running it on a single Linux server: `config/deploy.yml`. Everything runs in one container. Puma serves the web requests, Solid Queue runs the background jobs inside Puma, and SQLite stores the data. [kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy) terminates SSL with a certificate from Let's Encrypt. The databases and uploaded files live in a Docker volume, so they survive deploys.
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server (Ubuntu or Debian work well) you can SSH into as root, or as a user that can run Docker. Kamal installs Docker if it isn't there yet.
+- A hostname whose DNS record points at that server, with ports 80 and 443 open. Let's Encrypt uses them to issue the certificate.
+- An SMTP server. Fizzy has no passwords: people sign in with a link sent by email, so mail has to work before anyone can sign in, you included.
+- Docker on your own machine, to build the image.
+
+### Fill in your details
+
+Open `config/deploy.yml` and set:
+
+- `host` at the top: the hostname Fizzy will be served from. It's used as the SSH target, for the SSL certificate and for the links in emails and notifications. If you SSH into the server by IP rather than by that name, put the IP under `servers/web`.
+- `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT` and `SMTP_USERNAME`: the address mail is sent from and the mail server that sends it. STARTTLS on port 587 with plain authentication is the default. The commented settings cover the other common setups.
+
+The image is pushed to a registry that Kamal runs on your server, so you don't need an account with Docker Hub or GHCR. If you'd rather use one, the comments in `config/deploy.yml` show how.
+
+### Provide the secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` lists the secrets the app needs and reads each one from an environment variable of the same name. It's safe to commit since it holds no values. Export the values in the shell you deploy from:
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret)
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=your-smtp-password
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=... # from the command below
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=...
+```
+
+`SECRET_KEY_BASE` signs cookies and sessions. Generate it once and keep it: changing it signs everyone out. The VAPID keys identify your server for browser push notifications. Generate the pair with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner "puts WebPush.generate_key.to_hash"
+```
+
+Rather than exporting them by hand, you can have `.kamal/secrets` fetch them from a password manager. Kamal has [adapters](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/commands/secrets/) for 1Password, Bitwarden, LastPass and others, and the file has a commented example.
+
+### Deploy
+
+The first time:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This installs Docker on the server if needed, starts kamal-proxy, builds the image, pushes it, and boots Fizzy. On boot the container creates the SQLite databases and runs migrations. Once it's up, open `https://your-host`. Signing up is open while there are no accounts yet, so enter your email address and follow the link Fizzy mails you. From then on, sign-ups are closed and you invite people from within Fizzy.
+
+For every release after that:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Kamal builds and pushes the new image, boots the new container next to the old one, waits for it to pass the health check, then switches traffic over. The new version connects to the same volume, so the data stays put.
+
+### Day to day
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs     # follow the logs
+bin/kamal console  # a Rails console on the server
+bin/kamal dbc      # a SQLite console on the production database
+bin/kamal shell    # a bash shell in the running container
+```
+
+Your data is in the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume on the server, which holds the SQLite databases and the uploaded files under `/rails/storage`. Back it up regularly. For instance, from the server:
+
+```sh
+docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/data -v $PWD:/backup debian tar czf /backup/fizzy-storage.tgz -C /data .
+```
+
+### Tuning
+
+Puma runs one worker per CPU and Solid Queue one worker process per CPU. On a small server that's usually right, but you can set `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` in `config/deploy.yml` to change it. Set `MULTI_TENANT: "true"` to let anyone with the address sign up for their own account on your Fizzy.
+
+To move the background jobs into their own container on the same server, or onto a second server, add a `jobs` role that runs `bin/jobs` and set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: "false"` for the web role. That's a different setup from the one in `config/deploy.yml`, though: with SQLite, both containers would have to share the same volume on the same box.
